# BPO topics
1. Average Handle Time (AHT) 
Definition: AHT is the average total time an agent spends on a customer interaction, including talk 
time, hold time, and after-call work (ACW). It is calculated as: (Talk Time + Hold Time + ACW) ÷ Total 
Calls Handled.

Why It Matters: AHT directly affects staffing costs, queue wait times, and customer experience. A 
high AHT inflates headcount requirements; a too-low AHT can signal rushed calls and poor quality. 
Industry benchmark typically sits between 4–6 minutes for general service lines, varying by 
complexity. 

Management Strategies: 
● Conduct regular call calibration sessions to identify where agents lose time — excessive hold, 
repetitive verification, or unfamiliarity with tools. 
● Invest in knowledge base optimisation so agents retrieve information faster without placing 
customers on hold. 
● Set AHT targets by interaction type, not blanket averages, to avoid penalising complex 
queries. 

Q1 | DIAGNOSIS
Your team's AHT has increased by 45 seconds over the past two weeks. Calls, volume, and
product remain unchanged. Walk me through how you would identify the root cause.
Model Answer
Start by disaggregating AHT into its three components — talk time, hold time, and ACW — to identify where the
increase is concentrated. Pull the data at both team and individual agent level to determine whether this is a
systemic or isolated issue.
• If hold time is up: Investigate navigation issues, system slowness, or process complexity. Check if a recent tool
or system change is causing agents to put customers on hold more frequently.
• If talk time is up: Listen to call recordings for patterns — are agents spending more time explaining a new
policy, dealing with complex issues, or struggling with call control?
• If ACW is up: Check if a new CRM step, wrap code, or documentation requirement was introduced. Also look
at whether new agents are joining the team skewing the average.
• Agent-level analysis: If only 2–3 agents are driving the spike, the root cause is individual coaching gaps. If all
agents are affected equally, it is a process or system problem.
Present your findings to management with data visualization (trend chart by component) and a specific action plan
addressing the identified root cause

Q2 | COACHING DILEMMA
An agent's AHT is 30% above the team average, but their CSAT scores are the highest on the
floor. How do you address this with them?
Model Answer
This is a quality vs. efficiency trade-off, and it requires a balanced, data-led coaching conversation — not a
directive to simply 'be faster.'
• Acknowledge excellence first: Open by recognising their outstanding CSAT. This validates their approach and
sets a collaborative tone.
• Use call listening together: Identify specific moments in calls where time could be saved without sacrificing
quality — for example, reducing filler phrases, using shorter verification scripts, or leveraging keyboard
shortcuts for ACW.
• Set a collaborative target: Rather than imposing the team average, agree on a realistic improvement target
(e.g., reduce by 60–90 seconds over 4 weeks) that maintains their CSAT above threshold.
• Benchmark and replicate: Document what makes their quality so high and consider using them as a calibration
model or buddy coach for newer agents.
The goal is not to make them average — it is to make them efficient without losing what makes them exceptional.

Q3 | MANAGEMENT PRESSURE
Management wants you to reduce AHT by 20% in 30 days to improve capacity. What steps
would you take, and what risks would you flag?
Model Answer
A 20% AHT reduction in 30 days is an aggressive target. Your plan must be structured, data-driven, and include a
risk register for leadership.
• ACW reduction (quickest win): Introduce call summary templates, standardize wrap codes, and train agents on
efficient note-taking. This typically yields 30–60 seconds of savings with low risk.
• Hold time reduction: Audit what agents search for during holds. Improve knowledge base accessibility, add
quick-reference guides, and review frequently consulted resources for consolidation.
• Talk time improvement: Run call control workshops focusing on structured call openings, probing efficiently,
and confident closures. Use call listening examples.
• Risk — FCR impact: Rushing agents can cause incomplete resolutions, increasing repeat contacts and
negating capacity gains. Monitor FCR weekly as a lagging indicator.
• Risk — CSAT impact: Customers who feel rushed give lower satisfaction scores. Ensure quality audit scores
remain stable throughout the initiative.
Recommend presenting a phased plan: quick wins in week 1–2 (ACW/holds), and sustainable talk time coaching
in weeks 3–4, with clear metrics and checkpoints.

Q4 | REAL-TIME FLOOR
During a peak hour you notice 3 agents with AHT exceeding 15 minutes on simple queries.
What immediate action do you take?
Model Answer
Real-time floor management requires fast triage. The priority is resolving the immediate service level impact while
protecting the customer experience.
• Silent listen or barge-in: Immediately listen to at least one of the three calls without interrupting to assess
whether it is a knowledge gap, customer complexity, or system issue.
• System check: If all three agents are affected simultaneously, suspect a system or tool outage. Check with IT
or your WFM team for any known issues.
• Gentle floor intervention: If it is a knowledge gap, send a quick chat message or floor walk to the agent offering
support — do not disrupt the call.
• Queue management: Alert WFM of the potential service level risk. Consider pulling agents from lower-priority
tasks to supplement the queue.
Post-shift, run a debrief with the three agents individually. Identify if there is a training gap, a new query type
emerging, or a recurring system issue, and address it before the next peak period

