2. Time Management 
Definition: Time management in a leadership context refers to the structured prioritisation of tasks — 
for both the leader and the team — to maximise productive output within available hours. 
Why It Matters: A team executive who cannot model effective time management will struggle to meet 
SLAs, conduct coaching, and handle escalations simultaneously. Poor time management cascades 
into missed targets and agent burnout. 

Q1 | STAFFING CRISIS
You have 20 agents on shift, a heavy call queue, and 4 agents are on scheduled breaks. Two
agents call in sick. How do you manage the next 2 hours?
Model Answer
This is a real-time staffing gap scenario. The priority order is: protect service level, communicate transparently,
and escalate if needed.
• Immediate: Stagger the remaining scheduled breaks — delay non-critical breaks by 15–30 minutes to keep
more agents available during the queue spike.
• Voluntary overtime: Quickly canvas agents who are ending their shift or on off-days for voluntary coverage.
Even 2–3 additional agents can significantly reduce queue pressure.
• WFM escalation: Inform your Workforce Management team immediately. They can adjust real-time staffing,
trigger contingency plans, or move agents from lower-volume queues.
• Task suspension: Temporarily suspend non-call activities (coaching sessions, reports, admin) and redirect
those agents to the queue.
• Communication: Update the team on the situation and the temporary plan. Transparency reduces stress and
maintains team morale during the crisis.
Document the incident post-shift and review the contingency plan with HR/WFM to prevent recurrence.

Q2 | PRIORITY CONFLICT
You have a team huddle scheduled, a pending PIP document to complete, and a sudden spike
in call volume. How do you prioritize?
Model Answer
Prioritization in operations follows a clear hierarchy: live service > people development > administrative tasks.
• Service level first: A sudden volume spike directly impacts customer experience and SLA. Defer the huddle
and redirect your attention to the floor. Communicate the change to your team immediately.
• Huddle: Reschedule within the same day or early the next shift. Brief your team with a quick 3-minute standing
update if absolutely necessary, rather than cancelling entirely.
• PIP documentation: This is important but not time-sensitive to the hour. Complete it after service level
stabilizes — typically during post-peak or your planning time block.
The key skill demonstrated here is triage — knowing the difference between urgent and important, and
communicating changes proactively rather than reactively.

Q3 | AGENT PUNCTUALITY
An agent is repeatedly late returning from breaks, causing service level to dip. You've
mentioned it informally twice. What's your next step?
Model Answer
Informal conversations have not produced the desired behaviour change. The next step is a formal, documented
coaching intervention.
• Formal 1-on-1: Schedule a structured conversation with the agent. Present the data — specific dates, times,
and the service level impact. Be factual, not emotional.
• Understand root cause: Before applying consequences, explore whether there is a personal, health, or
workspace-related reason. Empathy is not an excuse for non-compliance, but it is context.
• Clear expectations: Restate the break schedule policy, the expected return time, and the consequence of
continued non-compliance (written warning / formal disciplinary process).
• Document: Record the conversation, the agreed expectations, and a review date in writing. Share a copy with
HR and the agent.
• Follow-up: Monitor adherence closely for the next 2 weeks. Acknowledge improvement or escalate to written
warning if the behaviour continues.

Q4 | SELF-MANAGEMENT
You manage coaching, reporting, escalations, and floor walking simultaneously each day.
Describe how you structure your time to ensure nothing falls through.
Model Answer
Effective self-management as a Team Leader requires deliberate time-blocking and disciplined prioritization.
• Pre-shift (15 mins): Review overnight reports, check staffing, flag any pending escalations or critical agent
issues for the day.
• Peak hours: Floor walking, real-time monitoring, and queue management. Coaching and reporting are
deprioritized during peak.
• Off-peak blocks: Dedicate 30-minute protected slots for 1-on-1 coaching sessions, call listening, and
performance reviews.
• Reporting: Use dashboards and automated reports to reduce manual data pulling. Set a fixed daily time slot
(e.g., post-peak) for report completion.
• Escalations: Handle immediately if critical (DSAT, complaint, policy breach). Route non-critical escalations to a
designated queue and batch-process them.
Delegation to senior agents or floor walkers for routine monitoring allows the TL to focus on higher-value activities
like coaching and development.

