3. Team Management 
Definition: Team management encompasses the day-to-day leadership of people — scheduling, 
motivation, conflict resolution, and building a culture of accountability and psychological safety. 
Why It Matters: Engaged, well-managed teams consistently outperform on every KPI. Gallup 
research links strong management to a 21% increase in profitability and significantly lower attrition. 
Management Strategies: 
● Hold regular 1-on-1s (bi-weekly minimum) to understand individual challenges, career goals, 
and blockers. 
● Set clear, measurable expectations using a performance scorecard visible to every team 
member. 
● Recognise performance publicly and address performance gaps privately and constructively.

Q1 | TEAM CONFLICT
Two top-performing agents are in a visible conflict that is affecting team morale. Neither wants
to escalate formally. How do you handle this?
Model Answer
Interpersonal conflict between high performers is particularly sensitive — mishandling it risks losing talent, not just
resolving a dispute.
• Separate 1-on-1s first: Meet each agent privately. Listen fully without judgment. Understand each perspective
before drawing any conclusions.
• Identify the root cause: Is it a workload issue, a perceived unfairness, a communication misunderstanding, or a
competition for recognition? The root cause determines the intervention.
• Joint mediation (with consent): If both parties are open to it, facilitate a structured joint conversation with
ground rules — focused on behaviour and impact, not personalities.
• Agree a working protocol: Co-create agreed behaviours for how they will interact professionally going forward.
Document this outcome.
• Monitor team impact: Check in with other team members informally to assess morale. Avoid letting the conflict
become a topic of team gossip.
Do not avoid this because they are top performers. Unresolved conflict normalizes toxic team dynamics and
accelerates attrition in the surrounding team.

Q2 | LOW MORALE
After a sudden process change, your team's engagement drops and several agents express
frustration publicly. What do you do?
Model Answer
Change resistance is a normal human response, especially when the 'why' behind the change has not been
communicated clearly. The TL's role is to be a credible bridge between leadership and the team.
• Acknowledge and validate: Hold a team session where you openly acknowledge that the change has caused
disruption. Never dismiss frustration — it escalates disengagement.
• Explain the 'why': Share the business rationale behind the change to the extent you are authorised to. Agents
who understand the purpose are significantly more likely to comply.
• Collect concerns with solutions: Invite agents to share specific concerns, but pair each concern with a
question: 'What would make this easier?' This shifts the energy from complaint to problem-solving.
• Escalate genuine concerns: If the feedback identifies real operational flaws in the new process, escalate to
management with data and proposed improvements — not just complaints.
• Recognize early adopters: Publicly acknowledge agents who adapt well and model the new behaviour. Peer
influence accelerates broader adoption.

Q3 | AGENT DEVELOPMENT
A high-potential agent is showing signs of disengagement and looking for growth. Your org
currently has no open TL roles. What do you do?
Model Answer
Page 9
Career growth does not require a promotion. The risk of inaction is losing a high performer to a competitor —
which costs significantly more than a creative retention strategy.
• Have an honest conversation: Ask directly what growth means to them. Is it more responsibility, a title change,
more pay, or different work? Their answer shapes your response.
• Stretch assignments: Give them ownership of a team project — floor walking shifts, buddy coaching, quality
calibration, or process improvement tasks.
• Subject Matter Expert (SME) role: Nominate them as the team's SME for a product, queue, or skill area. This
provides visibility and recognition without requiring a vacant TL role.
• Visible career pathway: Build a written development plan with milestones. Even if no role is available now, a
credible timeline ('TL pipeline, targeting Q3') demonstrates the organisation's investment.
• Cross-team visibility: Involve them in cross-functional projects or management meetings where they can
demonstrate their potential to senior stakeholders.

Q4 | SUDDEN UNDERPERFORMANCE
An agent who was once a consistent performer has declined sharply over 6 weeks. Metrics are
down, attendance is irregular. What is your approach?
Model Answer
A sudden performance change in a previously consistent agent almost always has an underlying cause. The first
step is to understand, not to judge.
• Private, empathetic 1-on-1: Create a safe space for the agent to share what is happening. Ask open questions:
'I've noticed some changes recently and I want to check in — how are you doing?'
• Explore root causes: Personal issues (health, family, financial stress), burnout, team conflict, or workplace
dissatisfaction could all be contributing. Each has a different intervention.
• Agree a support plan: If there is a personal issue, explore EAP (Employee Assistance Programme), flexible
scheduling, or a temporary adjusted target. Document the agreed support.
• Performance Improvement Plan (if no improvement): If after a reasonable support period there is no
improvement, move to a formal PIP with SMART targets, a support structure, and a review timeline.
The distinction between a support plan and a PIP is intent — a support plan is designed to help, while a PIP is a
formal performance management tool. Always exhaust the former before the latter/

