5. First Contact Resolution (FCR) 
Definition: FCR measures the percentage of customer issues resolved in a single interaction without 
requiring a callback or follow-up. Industry benchmark: 70–75% is considered average; best-in-class 
operations target 85%+. 
Why It Matters: FCR is one of the strongest predictors of customer satisfaction and cost efficiency. 
Each unresolved contact costs roughly double the original interaction in re-handling costs. 
Management Strategies: 
● Identify repeat contact drivers by tagging and analysing unresolved calls, then close 
knowledge or process gaps. 
● Empower agents with clear escalation authority and decision-making tools to resolve issues 
on the first call. 
● Incorporate FCR into quality scorecards so it's coached alongside soft skills. 

Q1 | ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS
Your team's FCR drops from 82% to 68% within a month. No product change was made. What is
your investigation plan?
Model Answer
A 14-point FCR drop is significant and warrants a structured root cause investigation before implementing any
solution.
• Categorize repeat contacts: Pull data on customers who called back within 48–72 hours for the same issue.
What are the top 3–5 repeat contact reasons? This is your primary diagnostic.
• Agent-level breakdown: Identify which agents have the lowest individual FCR rates. Is the drop concentrated
in specific agents (training/coaching issue) or uniform across the team (process/system issue)?
• New agent cohort analysis: If new hires joined recently, their lower FCR during ramping is often the driver.
Segment new vs. tenured agent FCR data separately.
• Call listening: Listen to 10–15 calls that resulted in a callback. Identify the moment of failure — was the issue
not resolved correctly, not fully confirmed, or was the agent avoiding complex queries?
• Knowledge base audit: Check if a policy, FAQ, or process guide was updated but not communicated to the
team, leaving agents with outdated information.
Present findings as a structured root cause report: category of failure, volume impact, agent(s) involved, and a
prioritized action plan with owners and timelines

Q2 | COACHING TRANSFERS
Page 11
An agent consistently transfers calls they could resolve themselves. This is hurting FCR. How
do you coach this behaviour?
Model Answer
Excessive transferring is typically driven by one of three root causes: knowledge gap, confidence deficit, or
avoidance. Each requires a different coaching approach.
• Identify the root cause first: Listen to 5–10 calls the agent transferred. Were they genuinely outside their skill
scope, or could the agent have resolved them with the right support?
• If it is a knowledge gap: Targeted upskilling sessions, knowledge base walkthroughs, and paired coaching with
a senior agent on those specific query types.
• If it is a confidence issue: Role-play scenarios in a safe environment. Build a 'first attempt checklist' the agent
can follow before deciding to transfer.
• If it is avoidance: Have a direct, honest conversation. Set a measurable target (e.g., reduce unnecessary
transfers by 50% in 2 weeks) and make it part of their coaching plan.
• Reward first-contact resolutions: Acknowledge when the agent resolves a complex query without transferring.
Positive reinforcement builds confidence faster than corrective feedback alone.

Q3 | PROCESS IMPROVEMENT
Customers are calling back within 48 hours for the same issue despite agents marking calls as
resolved. What process improvements would you suggest?
Model Answer
This pattern indicates a systemic issue with resolution quality or verification, not just individual agent behaviour.
• Improve call closure technique: Train agents on the 3-step close: summarise the resolution, confirm the
customer's understanding, and proactively address likely follow-up questions before the customer hangs up.
• Introduce a resolution confirmation step: Add a mandatory script line before ending the call — 'Is there
anything else I can help you with today, and are you happy that this issue is fully resolved?'
• CRM note quality: Audit ACW notes on repeat-contact calls. If notes are vague or incomplete, agents handling
the repeat contact cannot build on prior resolution attempts, causing duplication of effort.
• 48-hour repeat contact flag: Implement a system flag that alerts supervisors when a customer calls back within
48 hours for the same query type. This enables proactive coaching of the original agent.
• Calibration session: Run a team calibration using repeat-contact call examples. Define what 'fully resolved'
looks like vs. what constitutes an incomplete resolution

