6. CSAT and DSAT (Customer Satisfaction & 
Dissatisfaction) 
Definition: CSAT measures the percentage of customers rating their experience positively (typically 
4–5 on a 5-point scale); DSAT is the inverse — the proportion rating 1–2, signalling active 
dissatisfaction. Industry CSAT benchmark: 80–85%+ for service operations. 
Why It Matters: CSAT drives retention and NPS. DSAT is a leading indicator of churn — dissatisfied 
customers are four times more likely to defect to a competitor. Both directly reflect team performance 
and process quality. 
Management Strategies: 
● Review DSAT verbatims weekly to identify recurring pain points — product issues, long wait 
times, or agent behaviour. 
● Celebrate CSAT wins by sharing positive verbatims with the team to reinforce the right 
behaviours. 
● Set individual CSAT targets within team scorecards and link coaching plans to low-scoring 
interactions.

Q1 | SUDDEN CSAT DROP
CSAT drops by 12 points in a week across your team. Call volume and staffing are stable. What
is your action plan?
Model Answer
A 12-point drop in one week is a significant signal that something has changed in either the customer experience,
the agent behaviour, or the feedback environment.
• Verbatim analysis first: Pull all open-text survey comments from the affected period. Categorize the themes —
is the feedback about agent tone, wait times, resolution quality, or product issues?
• Correlate with agent data: Identify if the drop is uniform across the team or driven by 2–3 specific agents.
Agent-level CSAT breakdown is your most actionable diagnostic.
• Check for process or script changes: Was a new script, hold procedure, or escalation path introduced? Even
minor changes can significantly affect how customers feel during the interaction.
• Call listening with calibration: Pull 10–15 calls from the lowest-rated interactions. Listen with a quality
framework. Identify specific behaviours or moments that caused dissatisfaction.
• Urgent team calibration: Run a 30-minute team huddle using good vs. poor call examples. Reset expectations
on empathy, resolution, and call closure standards.
Present findings to management within 48 hours with a root cause summary and a 2-week recovery plan with
measurable milestones

Q2 | AHT vs CSAT TRADE-OFF
Page 13
One of your agents has the highest CSAT scores but also the highest AHT. How do you present
this to senior management?
Model Answer
This is a strategic discussion about the relationship between efficiency and quality — not a simple performance
problem.
• Frame it as a benchmark opportunity: This agent demonstrates what world-class customer experience looks
like. Their CSAT score is not coincidental — it is a result of specific behaviours that can be replicated.
• Analyse what drives their quality: Review their calls to identify the specific behaviours causing high CSAT —
empathy phrases, complete resolutions, proactive follow-up. These become your team coaching standards.
• Model the AHT improvement pathway: Present a coaching plan targeting the agent's AHT reduction in a
specific component (e.g., ACW, hold time) without changing what drives their CSAT.
• Business case for quality: Model the cost of repeat contacts prevented by high CSAT vs. the capacity cost of
higher AHT. In many cases, CSAT improvement delivers higher ROI than AHT reduction.
The message to management: 'This agent is our quality benchmark. We are working collaboratively to improve
their efficiency without compromising the customer experience that makes them exceptional.'

Q3 | SURVEY COACHING
Agents are suspected of coaching customers to give 5-star ratings before the survey is sent.
How do you address this?
Model Answer
Coaching customers on survey ratings is a form of data manipulation that undermines the organisation's ability to
make informed decisions. It must be addressed directly.
• Audit call recordings: Identify specific instances where agents are prompting ratings. This gives you factual
evidence rather than suspicion, which is essential for a fair conversation.
• Team education session: Without naming individuals initially, run a session explaining why authentic CSAT
matters — business decisions on process, training, and investment are made based on this data.
• Clear policy communication: State explicitly that coaching customers on survey responses is a policy violation
with specific consequences, and document this in the team's code of conduct.
• 1-on-1 with identified agents: Have a private, direct conversation with agents found to be coaching. Distinguish
between accidental (thanking the customer for their time) and deliberate (telling them to rate 5) behaviour.
• Reframe CSAT incentives: If CSAT is tied to bonuses in a way that creates pressure to manipulate, raise this
with management. Incentive design should reward genuine improvement, not gaming

Q1 | PATTERN ANALYSIS
Your DSAT rate spikes every Monday morning. Volume is the same as other days. What would
you investigate?
Model Answer
A day-specific DSAT pattern points to a structural or staffing issue tied to Monday conditions, not to agent
behaviour alone.
• Weekend backlog effect: Customers who had unresolved issues over the weekend arrive on Monday morning
already frustrated. The first agent they reach absorbs that frustration regardless of their performance.
• Post-weekend system issues: Are there known system delays, data sync issues, or maintenance windows that
affect Monday service quality? Cross-check with IT and your WFM team.
• Monday shift composition: Are less experienced agents, higher proportions of new hires, or reduced TL
coverage scheduled on Monday mornings? Staffing quality affects both resolution capability and tone.
• Self-service failure on weekends: If the IVR, chatbot, or website fails to resolve issues over the weekend,
customers arrive at the call centre on Monday with compounded frustration.
Action: Run a Monday-specific call audit, correlate DSAT verbatims with time-of-day data, and adjust staffing or
scheduling to add experienced agents on Monday mornings

Q2 | AGENT-SPECIFIC DSAT
A customer submits a DSAT with a verbatim comment naming a specific agent for being
dismissive. How do you handle this?
Model Answer
Page 15
A named DSAT requires a balanced approach — protecting the customer's experience while ensuring the agent is
treated fairly.
• Pull and listen to the call: Before any conversation with the agent, review the recording. Assess whether the
customer's perception of 'dismissiveness' aligns with the actual interaction.
• Assess objectively: Was the agent genuinely dismissive, or was the customer's interpretation shaped by
frustration about the issue itself? Both scenarios require different responses.
• Private coaching conversation: If the behaviour is validated, have a structured, non-punitive conversation. Play
the relevant section of the call and ask the agent to reflect — 'How do you think the customer felt at this point?'
• Define the expected standard: Use specific examples of better phrasing. Role-play alternative approaches. Set
a follow-up review date.
• Check for pattern: Is this an isolated incident or part of a trend? Pull the agent's last 30 DSAT calls for a trend
assessment before deciding on the level of intervention.
Document the feedback, the coaching conversation, and the agreed improvement actions in the agent's
performance record.

Q3 | SYSTEMIC DSAT
DSAT verbatim feedback consistently mentions long hold times and unresolved issues, not
agent behaviour. What do you do?
Model Answer
When DSAT is driven by systemic failures rather than agent behaviour, coaching agents will not fix the problem.
The intervention must be at the operational or process level.
• Build the business case: Compile the DSAT data, categorized verbatims, and trend over time into a clear
presentation. Quantify the impact — repeat contacts generated, customer churn risk, revenue at stake.
• Identify the root causes: Long hold times suggest navigation issues, tool accessibility, or knowledge gaps.
Unresolved issues suggest empowerment gaps (agents lacking authority to resolve) or process complexity.
• Propose specific solutions: Do not just escalate the problem — attach proposed fixes. For hold times: improve
knowledge base, add quick-reference tools. For unresolved issues: review escalation thresholds, expand agent
authority.
• Protect your agents: Make it explicit to management that the DSAT is a process or system failure, not an agent
performance failure. Coaching agents for problems they cannot control damages morale and trust.
This situation demonstrates operational maturity — the ability to distinguish between agent-driven and
system-driven problems is one of the most valued skills in a senior TL or operations manager role.

