7. Performance Management 
Definition: Performance management is the continuous cycle of goal-setting, monitoring, feedback, 
and development that ensures every team member meets expected standards and grows 
professionally. 
Why It Matters: Without a structured performance framework, underperformance festers and top 
performers disengage. A clear, fair system protects the business legally and culturally. 
Management Strategies: 
● Use a balanced scorecard covering productivity (AHT, volume), quality (CSAT, FCR), and 
behaviour (attendance, adherence). 
● Follow a PIPs (Performance Improvement Plans) process with documented milestones, 
support resources, and review dates. 
● Differentiate management of top, mid, and low performers — develop, retain, and correct 
respectively.

Q1 | PIP CONVERSATION
You need to put an agent on a Performance Improvement Plan. They are defensive and deny
there is an issue. How do you conduct this conversation?
Model Answer
A PIP conversation with a resistant agent requires careful preparation, emotional regulation, and an unwavering
focus on data over opinion.
• Prepare with data: Enter the conversation with specific, documented evidence — metric reports, call scores,
attendance records. Never rely on general impressions.
• Open with structure, not accusation: 'I want to talk through your performance data over the last 8 weeks and
share some concerns I have.' Avoid emotional language like 'you have been letting the team down.'
• Acknowledge their perspective: If the agent pushes back, say 'I hear that you see it differently. Can you walk
me through your view?' Listen genuinely — there may be context you are missing.
• Return to data: After acknowledging, redirect: 'I understand your perspective, and I want to make sure we also
look at the data together.' Reviewing the data together is more effective than presenting conclusions.
• SMART goals and support: Frame the PIP as a structured support tool, not a disciplinary end-point. Agree
specific, measurable targets, the support you will provide, and the review timeline.
Close by asking: 'What support do you need from me to achieve these targets?' This shifts the dynamic from
punitive to collaborative, which improves the probability of success.

Q2 | INCONSISTENT PERFORMANCE
An agent performs well when you are present but underperforms when you are away. How do
you address this?
Model Answer
This behaviour pattern — sometimes called 'supervisor effect' — indicates the agent is capable of meeting
standards but is choosing not to when accountability is perceived as lower.
• Lead with data from all shifts: Present performance data across observed and unobserved shifts side-by-side.
This makes the pattern undeniable without relying on subjective observation.
• Discuss accountability and self-management: 'The standard I need from you is the same whether I am on the
floor or not. Your metrics should reflect what you are capable of, not who is watching.'
• Use remote monitoring tools: Implement structured call listening from unobserved shifts. Share findings in
coaching to show the agent that performance is always visible, not just when you are physically present.
• Peer accountability: Consider a buddy or senior agent check-in system during your absence. This distributes
accountability without requiring your constant physical presence.
• Formal follow-up: If the pattern continues after the conversation, document it as a performance pattern and
initiate a structured coaching plan with clear consequences

Q3 | TEAM-WIDE KPI MISS
Page 17
Your entire team misses KPIs for two consecutive months. Your manager asks you what went
wrong. How do you respond?
Model Answer
The response to a team-wide performance failure must demonstrate accountability, analytical thinking, and a
credible recovery plan.
• Own accountability: Begin by acknowledging your role as the team leader. Avoid deflecting to external factors
as the primary explanation, even if they contributed.
• Present a structured root cause analysis: Categorize the contributing factors — staffing changes,
product/process changes, training gaps, morale factors, or WFM planning issues. Use data to support each
factor.
• Distinguish controllable from uncontrollable: Some factors (system outages, volume spikes) are outside your
control. Others (coaching frequency, calibration quality, agent motivation) are within it. Be honest about both.
• Present a recovery plan: Outline specific actions, owners, timelines, and success metrics. A recovery plan
must be time-bound — 'we will return to target within 6 weeks' with weekly milestones.
The key message: 'I take responsibility for the team's performance. Here is what I believe contributed, what I am
doing about it, and how I will ensure it does not recur.' Managers respond to accountability paired with
competence.

Q4 | RECOGNITION DILEMMA
You have budget for one 'best performer' reward this month, but two agents are tied on all
metrics. What do you do?
Model Answer
This tests your judgment on fairness, team culture, and the purpose of recognition — not just metric-reading.
• Look beyond metrics: When quantitative scores are tied, examine qualitative factors — consistency over the
month vs. a late surge, team contribution (helping colleagues, mentoring), attendance, and attitude during
difficult periods.
• Consult quality scores: If QA scores differ between the two, use this as the tiebreaker. Quality is often
under-weighted in pure metric comparisons.
• Split recognition creatively: If you genuinely cannot differentiate, consider one formal award and one public
recognition (shoutout in team meeting, message to senior manager). Both agents feel valued without the budget
being exceeded.
• Be transparent: Briefly explain to the team how the decision was made. Transparency builds trust in the
recognition system and reduces resentment.
Avoid flipping a coin or defaulting to seniority. The recognition process is a leadership moment — how you decide
communicates what your team values and what behaviours you reward

