8. Shrinkage and Shrinkage Formula 
Definition: Shrinkage is the percentage of scheduled time during which agents are unavailable to 
handle contacts, including breaks, training, meetings, absenteeism, and system downtime. 
Formula: Shrinkage % = (Total Hours Lost ÷ Total Hours Scheduled) × 100 
Industry benchmark: 25–35% is typical; exceeding 40% signals a structural workforce problem. 
Why It Matters: Shrinkage directly determines how many agents must be scheduled to hit service 
level targets. Under-accounting for shrinkage results in understaffing, long queues, and SLA 
breaches. 

Q1 | CALCULATION
Your centre has 50 agents working 8-hour shifts. Daily shrinkage averages 22%. How many
productive hours are actually available?
Model Answer
This is a direct application of the shrinkage formula.
• Total scheduled hours: 50 agents x 8 hours = 400 hours.
• Shrinkage hours lost: 400 x 22% = 88 hours unavailable.
• Productive hours available: 400 - 88 = 312 hours.
• Staffing implication: Workforce Management uses this to determine how many agents to roster vs. how many
are actually needed on the phones. If you need 400 productive hours, you would need to schedule
approximately 513 hours worth of agents (400 / 0.78) to account for the shrinkage.
Understanding shrinkage helps TLs and WFM avoid the common mistake of equating headcount with capacity. A
team of 50 agents is not the same as 50 agents available to handle contacts

Q2 | REDUCING UNPLANNED SHRINKAGE
Page 19
Your unplanned shrinkage has risen to 18% versus a target of 10%. What steps do you take to
bring it down?
Model Answer
Unplanned shrinkage is largely driven by absenteeism, late logins, and extended breaks. Each has a distinct
intervention.
• Root cause breakdown: Analyse unplanned shrinkage by category. Is the primary driver absenteeism, late
logins, or something else? The intervention must match the cause.
• Attendance management: Implement a clear return-to-work process for absences. Review patterns (same day
each week? after specific shifts?) to identify potential disengagement or burnout signals.
• Schedule adherence tools: Use real-time adherence dashboards to flag agents who are off-queue without
authorisation. Address immediately rather than at end of shift.
• Engagement interventions: High unplanned shrinkage often signals low engagement or burnout. Run stay
interviews and team pulse checks to identify root causes beyond logistics.
• Incentivise attendance: Where HR policy allows, recognise teams or individuals with strong attendance
records. A positive reinforcement approach is often more effective than punitive measures alone.
Target the 8% gap (18% vs 10%) in phases: address the top 1-2 categories driving the most hours first, then
tackle secondary factors.

Q3 | TRAINING SCHEDULING
You need to plan a 2-hour mandatory product training for 30 agents during a high-volume day.
How do you minimise the shrinkage impact?
Model Answer
Planning training on a high-volume day requires careful batching, timing, and WFM coordination.
• Batch into groups: Split 30 agents into 3 groups of 10. Each group attends a 2-hour session in rotation,
ensuring 20 agents remain available at any given time.
• Schedule during low-volume windows: Use historical intraday volume data to identify the quietest 2-hour
windows (typically mid-morning or early afternoon on most campaigns). Schedule each batch there.
• WFM coordination: Flag the training plan to WFM in advance. They can adjust the staffing model, flag the
shrinkage uplift, and arrange contingency coverage if needed.
• Pre-brief agents: Ensure agents know the schedule in advance so break and lunch patterns can be adjusted
around training slots.
Document the planned vs. actual shrinkage impact post-training. This data helps improve future training
scheduling decisions

