9. Attrition 
Definition: Attrition is the rate at which employees leave the organisation (voluntary and involuntary) 
over a given period. Formula: (Leavers ÷ Average Headcount) × 100. Contact centre industry 
average: 30–45% annually, though best-in-class operations target below 20%. 
Why It Matters: Each agent departure costs an estimated 20–40% of annual salary in recruitment, 
onboarding, and productivity loss. High attrition destabilises team performance and erodes 
institutional knowledge.

Q1 | SUDDEN EXITS
Three of your best agents resign in the same month citing 'lack of growth.' What immediate and
long-term actions do you take?
Model Answer
Three simultaneous resignations from high performers is a critical retention signal that requires both immediate
damage control and a structural response.
• Immediate — stay interviews: Within the next 5 business days, hold confidential 1-on-1 stay interviews with
every remaining team member. Ask: 'What keeps you here? What would make you consider leaving?'
• Immediate — retention conversations: Identify the next 3–5 agents most at risk of leaving based on tenure,
engagement signals, and informal feedback. Have proactive retention conversations.
• Escalate with data: Present the 3 exits to HR and senior management with the 'lack of growth' theme as the
stated cause. Frame it as a systemic risk, not 3 isolated incidents.
• Long term — visible career paths: Work with HR to build and communicate a documented growth pathway —
from agent to senior agent, SME, TL, quality coach, and beyond — with clear criteria for each step.
• Long term — stretch opportunities: While formal promotions are created, implement SME roles, project
ownership, and cross-functional secondments as immediate growth outlets.
The cost of replacing a high performer is typically 1.5–2x their annual salary when you factor in recruitment,
onboarding, and performance ramp time. Retention investment is always cheaper.

Q2 | EXIT DATA
Page 21
Exit interview data shows 60% of agents who left cited 'team leader behaviour' as the primary
reason. You are the TL. How do you respond?
Model Answer
This is one of the most personally challenging feedback scenarios a TL can face. The response must demonstrate
self-awareness, professionalism, and a genuine commitment to change.
• Do not get defensive: Defensive responses in this context signal a lack of self-awareness, which compounds
the original problem. The appropriate initial response is: 'Thank you for sharing this with me. I want to
understand it better.'
• Request specific examples: Ask HR for specific, anonymised verbatims from exit interviews. General feedback
like 'team leader behaviour' is not actionable without specifics.
• Self-audit: Review your recent interactions, coaching conversations, communication style, and any decisions
that may have caused friction. Be honest about what you find.
• 360-degree feedback: Request a structured 360 from your current team (anonymous format through HR) to
identify patterns. This demonstrates courage and a genuine desire to improve.
• Coaching from your manager: Engage your own line manager or an HR business partner for coaching. Model
the same development behaviours you expect from your agents.
The TLs who respond to this feedback with accountability and a structured development plan are the ones who
build the strongest long-term teams. This scenario is a professional growth opportunity, not a career-ending event.

Q3 | HIGH ATTRITION QUARTER
Your attrition is 25% this quarter versus a 12% target. Your manager asks you to present a
recovery plan. What does it include?
Model Answer
A recovery plan for high attrition must address root causes, not just symptoms, and must be credible to leadership
with specific timelines and metrics.
• Attrition breakdown: Segment by voluntary/involuntary, regretted/non-regretted, tenure band (new hire vs.
tenured agents), and shift pattern. Each segment has different drivers and interventions.
• Root cause analysis: Based on exit interviews and stay interview data, identify the top 3 drivers. Common
causes: pay competitiveness, management relationship, workload/burnout, lack of growth, commute/flexibility.
• Hiring pipeline: High attrition requires a parallel response on the supply side. Work with TA (Talent Acquisition)
to accelerate the pipeline, improve quality of hire screening, and strengthen onboarding to reduce early-tenure
exits.
• Engagement interventions: Launch a structured engagement programme — recognition schemes, flexible
scheduling, team social activities, and transparent communication about the organisation's direction.
• Short-term metrics: Set weekly attrition tracking with leading indicators (resignation notices received,
employees at risk flagged in stay interviews, engagement pulse scores).
Present the plan in 3 horizons: 30-day quick wins (engagement, stay interviews, pipeline), 60-day structural
changes (career paths, manager coaching), 90-day target (return to 12% annualised rate).

Q4 | RETENTION CONVERSATION
A high performer tells you informally they have received an external offer. You cannot match
the salary. What do you do?
Model Answer
A salary counter-offer that cannot be matched forces a conversation about total value, not just base pay. Research
consistently shows that counter-offers without structural change result in the agent leaving within 6 months
anyway.
• Listen first: Before proposing anything, understand the full picture. Is the external offer purely about money, or
are there other factors — role type, flexibility, commute, culture?
• Explore non-monetary value: Map what your organisation offers that the external role may not — stability,
team relationships, career path, brand, work-life balance, benefits, and development opportunities.
• Make a credible case for the future: If a promotion or pay review is genuinely on the horizon (within 3–6
months), present this specifically with a timeline. Vague promises accelerate exits, not retention.
• Be honest: If you cannot match the offer and cannot offer a near-term improvement, say so directly.
Respecting the agent's decision builds trust and may result in a positive departure — they are more likely to
return or refer others.
• Escalate if possible: If the agent is genuinely critical to the team, escalate to HR or your manager immediately
with the business case for a salary exception.
Document the conversation regardless of outcome. If they stay, set a 30-day check-in. If they leave, use the
information to strengthen your retention strategy for the rest of the team.

